\section{Controls}{

A top-level assignment whose right-hand side is a \strong{plain value} becomes a
control on the block, meaning a literal, a \code{c()} of literals, or a call to
\code{factor()} or \code{as.Date()}. Everything else is code.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{.vars <- intersect(c("AGE", "SEX", "TRT"), names(data))  # private, no control

vars <- factor(.vars, levels = .vars)                # a multi-select
species <- factor("setosa", levels(data$Species))    # a dropdown
n <- 10                                              # a number box
desc <- TRUE                                         # a checkbox

data |>
  dplyr::filter(Species \%in\% species) |>
  dplyr::slice_head(n = n)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A \strong{factor} is how a select is expressed: its levels are the choice list,
and the length of its value decides single or multiple. For a text column
write \code{sort(unique(data$col))} as the levels. \code{unique()} alone follows the
order of the rows, and the control is drawn again whenever that changes.
A pick whose level is no longer in the data is not used: the control goes
back to the value the script wrote.

A name starting with a dot is never a control. A name the script assigns
again is not one either, since only the last assignment could be.

The script reads a control as its current value, written into the code as
a literal. Three things follow, all by design:
\itemize{
\item A select's pick is a string (a character vector for a multi-select),
not a factor. \code{levels(x)} or \code{as.integer(x)} on a control do not give
what they give for the factor on the declaration line.
\item Every read of the control's name is replaced, also inside data-masking
calls. With a control \code{n}, \code{dplyr::filter(n > 5)} compares the control
and not a column called \code{n}. Write \code{.data$n} for the column. \code{.env$n}
does not reach the control: its line is no longer in the code that runs.
\item A later declaration reads an earlier control at its current value, so
the choices of one dropdown can depend on the pick in another:
\code{col <- factor("SEX", names(data))} followed by
\code{val <- factor(character(0), sort(unique(data[[col]])))}.
}

The optional \verb{#|} annotation carries what a value cannot say, such as a
numeric range or a label: \code{n <- 10  #| number(min = 1, max = 50)}.
}

\section{Sentence}{

\verb{#| title:}, \verb{#| subtitle:} and \verb{#| caption:} lines give the block a
sentence whose words are its controls, as blockr.viz's blocks do:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{arm <- factor("Placebo", levels = sort(unique(data$TRT01A)))
top_n <- 8  #| number(min = 3, max = 15)
serious <- FALSE  #| label = "serious events only"
#| title: Top \{@top_n\} adverse events in \{@arm\}
#| subtitle: Subjects per term[, \{@serious\}]
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\verb{\{@arg\}} prints a control's value and makes the word clickable: a select
opens its list, a number or text becomes a field, a checkbox toggles. A
clause in \verb{[ ... ]} leaves when its value is empty, and its control is
offered as a \code{+ label} chip instead. \code{{n}}, \code{{label(col)}} and \code{{col}} read
the first input. The sentence sits in the row with the gear; the controls
it names leave the grid, the others stay. A date can be named but stays a
control in the grid. The sentence is shown above any result and is not
part of the exported code.
}

\section{Exported code}{

The current values are written into the body as literals, so the exported
code has no assignment left for a control.

blockr.core fills the input names in with \code{bquote()}, which also evaluates
every \code{.()} and \code{..()} the script calls itself, such as data.table's
\code{.(x)}. Write \code{list(x)} there. The editor footer names the line.
}

\section{Saved as lines}{

A saved board writes the script as a JSON array, one element per line, so a
change to the script shows up in a git diff as the lines that changed.
}

